  "prompt_text": "This figure is a network-style summary linking several hypothetical particle types in the center to the experimental channels and observables around the edges that constrain them. The middle column lists new-physics candidates such as VLQ, LQ, VLL, S, Z′, W′, DQ, and G′. Colored arrows connect these particles to measurements or anomaly channels, showing which models can potentially address or are tested by each signature.\n\nThe external boxes include flavor and collider observables such as neutrino interactions, \\(R(D^{(*)})\\), the electron and muon anomalous magnetic moments, the \\(W\\)-mass measurement, proton–proton to \\(e^+e^-\\), rare \\(b \\to s\\ell^+\\ell^-\\) decays, dijet production, and muon-related channels. Several arrows converge on scalar \\(S\\) and \\(Z′\\), indicating that these two appear to be relevant to many different observables. Leptoquarks and vector-like leptons also connect to multiple flavor anomalies, while diquarks and color-octet-like states near the bottom are mainly tied to dijet and related collider constraints.\n\nOverall, the graphic conveys the interplay between proposed beyond-Standard-Model particles and a broad set of precision, flavor, and collider measurements, highlighting where explanations overlap and where constraints compete.",
